.TH PAXUTILS 1 "paxutils" "paxutils General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
paxutils \- unified core utilities multi-call binary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paxutils
.I command
[...]
.br
.B cat
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B chmod
.I OCTAL_MODE FILE...
.br
.B chown
.I USER[:GROUP] FILE...
.br
.B clear
.br
.B cp
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
.I SOURCE DEST
.br
.B echo
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fISTRING\fR...]
.br
.B env
[\fB\-i\fR] [\fINAME=VALUE\fR...] [\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR...]]
.br
.B grep
.I PATTERN
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B head
[\fB\-n\fR \fILINES\fR]
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B kill
[\fB\-\fR\fISIGNAL\fR] \fIPID\fR...
.br
.B less
[\fB\-\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B ln
[\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
.I TARGET LINK_NAME
.br
.B ls
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-l\fR]
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B mkdir
[\fB\-p\fR | \fB\-P\fR | \fB\-\-panic\fR]
.I DIRECTORY...
.br
.B mv
.I SOURCE DEST
.br
.B ps
.br
.B pwd
.br
.B rm
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
.I FILE...
.br
.B rmdir
.I DIRECTORY...
.br
.B sleep
[\fB\-w\fR]
[\fISECONDS\fR]
.br
.B tail
[\fB\-n\fR \fILINES\fR]
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B tee
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B touch
\fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B truncate
.B \-s
.I SIZE FILE...
.br
.B uname
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-m\fR]
.br
.B yes
[\fISTRING\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paxutils
is a single-binary core utility collection. It implements essential userland POSIX commands.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B cat
Concatenate FILE(s) or standard input to standard output.
.TP
.B chmod
Change file permissions utilizing a base-8 octal mode string parser loop. Fail early if malformed.
.TP
.B chown
Change file owner and group via user database lookups without heap allocation.
.TP
.B clear
Clear the terminal screen and scrollback buffer using raw ANSI escape codes.
.TP
.B cp
Copy files or directories (supports recursive copy with \-r and force unlinking with \-f).
.TP
.B echo
Print text to standard output.
.TP
.B env
Display active environmental variables, or run a binary within a modified environment footprint. Supports \-i to wipe the environmental vector space entirely.
.TP
.B grep
Pax Regular Expression Printer. A clean, allocation-free text filtering stream scanner that matches tracking strings line-by-line via optimized sub-pointer arrays.
.TP
.B head
Output the first part of files (default 10 lines).
.TP
.B kill
Send operating system scheduling signals (e.g., \-9 / \-KILL) straight to target PIDs via kernel routines.
.TP
.B less
Smart ANSI-aware terminal pager. Auto-wraps long text chunks to match 85-column parameters, tracks printable string widths, and passes raw color arrays dynamically to Alacritty.
.TP
.B ln
Make links between files (supports \-s for symbolic links and \-f to overwrite existing links).
.TP
.B ls
List directory contents with automatic color highlighting for directories and executables.
.TP
.B mkdir
Create directories. Supports standard POSIX recursive parent path mapping via \-p. Passing \-P or \-\-panic triggers Panic Mode, causing an early system error if a collision triggers.
.TP
.B mv
Move or rename files and directories.
.TP
.B ps
Process snapshot utility. Parses live system task attributes dynamically from the virtual /proc filesystem registers using flat stack frames.
.TP
.B pwd
Print name of current/working directory.
.TP
.B rm
Remove files or directories. Armed with an explicit root preservation layout script that captures and blocks system-wiping attempts with a definitive "No." message.
.TP
.B rmdir
Remove empty directories.
.TP
.B sleep
Pause execution for a specified number of seconds. Hybrid Feature: Passing \-w triggers DOS-style keypress pause mode ("Press any key to continue . . .").
.TP
.B tail
Output the last part of files (default 10 lines).
.TP
.B tee
High-throughput data stream T-splitter pipe. Duplicates standard input, flushing blocks down to stdout and disk files simultaneously via static 4KB stream chunk arrays.
.TP
.B touch
Initialize empty file sandboxes, or update filesystem node modification timestamps atomically.
.TP
.B truncate
Shrink or extend the size of each FILE to the specified size.
.TP
.B uname
Print system information. Implements POSIX-standard flags querying kernel system info directly via utsname.
.TP
.B yes
Output a string repeatedly until killed. Optimized with a 64KB chunked memory buffer for extreme I/O throughput.

